  ($qb$S16C01Q01$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The organisation's stated values (written on the wall, website, documents) are:$qb$, $qb$Genuinely lived — employee behaviour consistently reflects the stated values$qb$, $qb$Generally lived — values are broadly reflected in how people work$qb$, $qb$Partially lived — values are referenced but behaviour does not consistently match them$qb$, $qb$Not lived — the stated values are widely seen as aspirational or performative$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C01Q02$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The gap between the organisation's stated culture and the actual lived culture is:$qb$, $qb$Minimal — what leadership says about the culture matches what employees experience$qb$, $qb$Small — some gaps exist but they are not significant enough to damage trust$qb$, $qb$Significant — employees regularly experience a disconnect between the stated and lived culture$qb$, $qb$Very large — the gap between stated and lived culture is a source of deep cynicism$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C01Q03$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The sense of shared purpose (why the organisation exists and why it matters) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — employees feel a genuine connection to the organisation's purpose and it energises their work$qb$, $qb$Present — purpose is articulated and most employees can connect to it$qb$, $qb$Weak — purpose exists as a statement but does not resonate in day-to-day work$qb$, $qb$Absent — employees feel the organisation lacks a meaningful purpose beyond financial results$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C01Q04$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Pride in working for the organisation in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — employees are genuinely proud and this translates into advocacy and discretionary effort$qb$, $qb$Generally present — most employees feel a degree of pride$qb$, $qb$Mixed — some feel proud; others do not feel a strong connection to the organisation$qb$, $qb$Low — employees are generally not proud advocates of the organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C01Q05$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The role of culture in the organisation's strategy in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Central — culture is explicitly identified as a strategic priority and invested in accordingly$qb$, $qb$Acknowledged — leadership recognises culture as important though investment is not always aligned$qb$, $qb$Stated — culture is mentioned but not backed by concrete investment or accountability$qb$, $qb$Absent — culture is not treated as a strategic priority or business lever$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C01Q06$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Culture consistency across different departments, teams, and levels in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — the culture feels consistent regardless of where you are in the organisation$qb$, $qb$Generally consistent — some variation exists between teams or departments$qb$, $qb$Variable — culture varies significantly between parts of the organisation$qb$, $qb$Very variable — the organisation effectively has multiple sub-cultures that do not share a common identity$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C01Q07$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The culture of the organisation supports the strategy:$qb$, $qb$Tightly — the culture actively enables the things the strategy requires$qb$, $qb$Generally — the culture is broadly aligned with the strategy$qb$, $qb$Partially — in some areas culture supports the strategy; in others it impedes it$qb$, $qb$Poorly — the culture is a significant impediment to executing the strategy$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C01Q08$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$How the organisation responds when culture violations occur in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Consistently and decisively — breaches of the culture are addressed regardless of who commits them$qb$, $qb$Generally — most significant culture violations are addressed$qb$, $qb$Inconsistently — culture violations are sometimes addressed and sometimes tolerated depending on seniority$qb$, $qb$Poorly — culture violations by senior people are systematically tolerated undermining the entire culture$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C01Q09$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The culture's openness to change and new ideas in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — the culture embraces change and new ideas as essential to growth$qb$, $qb$Positive — the culture is generally open to change$qb$, $qb$Cautious — the culture is somewhat resistant to change and new approaches$qb$, $qb$Resistant — the culture actively resists change and this is a significant organisational constraint$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C01Q10$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Overall, the culture identity and values alignment in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is a genuine competitive advantage — the culture is a talent attractor and performance driver$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — the culture is reasonably positive and supports performance$qb$, $qb$Has notable gaps — culture inconsistency or misalignment is limiting the organisation's potential$qb$, $qb$Is a serious concern — the culture is actively harming performance, talent, and strategy execution$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C02Q01$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Psychological safety in your organisation (the ability to speak up, disagree, and make mistakes without punishment) is:$qb$, $qb$High — employees genuinely feel safe to express views, challenge decisions, and admit errors$qb$, $qb$Generally present — most employees feel safe in most situations$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — psychological safety varies significantly by team and manager$qb$, $qb$Low — fear of speaking up is a dominant experience across the organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C02Q02$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The experience of raising concerns or disagreeing with a manager in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Safe and welcomed — dissenting views are valued and managers actively seek challenge$qb$, $qb$Generally safe — most managers are open to challenge even if they do not always agree$qb$, $qb$Uncertain — some managers are open; others react badly to challenge$qb$, $qb$Unsafe — raising concerns with managers is likely to result in negative consequences$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C02Q03$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Employee experience when they make an honest mistake in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Psychological safety is intact — mistakes are treated as learning, not punished$qb$, $qb$Generally manageable — mistakes are usually handled professionally$qb$, $qb$Anxiety-inducing — mistakes create significant fear even when they are honest and minor$qb$, $qb$Very punitive — employees fear the consequences of mistakes and hide errors as a result$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C02Q04$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Bad news travels upward in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Freely — employees and managers report problems promptly without fear of the messenger being shot$qb$, $qb$Generally — most bad news reaches leadership in a timely way$qb$, $qb$Slowly — bad news is filtered or delayed as it moves up the organisation$qb$, $qb$Very slowly or not at all — bad news is systematically suppressed and leadership is often the last to know$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C02Q05$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The culture of open debate and constructive challenge in meetings in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — meetings are genuinely characterised by open debate and challenge$qb$, $qb$Generally present — most meetings allow for constructive discussion$qb$, $qb$Weak — meetings are characterised by agreement and deference rather than genuine debate$qb$, $qb$Very weak — challenging ideas in a meeting, particularly the boss's ideas, is widely avoided$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C02Q06$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Retaliation (formal or informal) against employees who raise concerns or speak up in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Absent — there is a strong culture of non-retaliation and this is enforced$qb$, $qb$Rare — retaliation is very uncommon and is addressed when it occurs$qb$, $qb$Known to occur — some employees have experienced informal consequences for speaking up$qb$, $qb$Common — retaliation against those who speak up is a known organisational problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C02Q07$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The experience of new joiners (whether they feel they can speak up before they have established credibility) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Positive — new joiners quickly feel they can contribute and challenge$qb$, $qb$Generally positive — new joiners feel welcome though it takes time to feel fully included$qb$, $qb$Slow — it takes new joiners significantly longer to feel safe to speak up than it should$qb$, $qb$Very slow — the organisation has a strong hierarchy of who is allowed to voice views$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C02Q08$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$How senior leaders respond to being challenged in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$With genuine openness — senior leaders model intellectual humility and welcome challenge$qb$, $qb$Generally well — most senior leaders handle challenge professionally$qb$, $qb$Variably — some leaders welcome challenge; others do not$qb$, $qb$Poorly — senior leader defensiveness to challenge is a significant contributor to low psychological safety$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C02Q09$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Overall psychological safety in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is a genuine competitive strength — the culture of openness accelerates learning and innovation$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — employees feel sufficiently safe to do good work$qb$, $qb$Has notable gaps — pockets of low safety are limiting performance in specific areas$qb$, $qb$Is a serious concern — widespread fear is significantly limiting performance, innovation, and talent retention$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C02Q10$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The visible consequences of speaking up (positive or negative) in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Positively reinforce speaking up — employees who raise concerns are seen to be valued$qb$, $qb$Are generally neutral — speaking up neither helps nor harms careers$qb$, $qb$Are sometimes negative — some employees have been visibly damaged by speaking up$qb$, $qb$Are systematically negative — the organisation's culture makes it clear that speaking up carries risk$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C03Q01$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The speed of decision-making in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Fast — decisions are made at the right level with minimal unnecessary escalation$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — decisions are made within a reasonable timeframe$qb$, $qb$Slow — the organisation is cautious and slow in its decision-making$qb$, $qb$Very slow — decision paralysis is a persistent and significant cultural problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C03Q02$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The quality of decisions in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — decisions are well-reasoned, evidence-based, and consistently good$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — most decisions are reasonable$qb$, $qb$Variable — decision quality varies significantly across the organisation$qb$, $qb$Poor — the quality of decisions is regularly questioned and decisions are often reversed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C03Q03$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The degree of centralisation in decision-making in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Well-calibrated — decisions are made at the right level and leaders are appropriately empowered$qb$, $qb$Generally appropriate — most decisions are made at an appropriate level$qb$, $qb$Too centralised — excessive escalation to senior leadership creates bottlenecks$qb$, $qb$Very centralised — almost all significant decisions require senior leadership approval$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C03Q04$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Inclusion of the right people in decisions in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Consistent — decision-making processes reliably include those with relevant knowledge and those who will be affected$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — most decisions involve the right people$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — some decisions are made without the involvement of key stakeholders$qb$, $qb$Poor — decisions are regularly made in ways that exclude people who should be involved$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C03Q05$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The culture of evidence-based decision-making in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — data and evidence are central to how decisions are made at all levels$qb$, $qb$Generally present — relevant data is used in most significant decisions$qb$, $qb$Weak — decisions are often made primarily on intuition without adequate data support$qb$, $qb$Very weak — evidence-based decision-making is not a cultural norm$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C03Q06$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The willingness to revisit and change poor decisions in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — the organisation learns fast and changes course when evidence shows a decision was wrong$qb$, $qb$Generally present — decisions are revisited when there is clear evidence of a problem$qb$, $qb$Low — reversing decisions is seen as weakness and is avoided even when the decision is clearly wrong$qb$, $qb$Very low — sunk cost thinking prevents the organisation from changing direction when needed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C03Q07$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The visibility of decision rationale (whether people understand why decisions were made) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — decision-makers communicate their rationale consistently and employees understand why$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — major decisions come with explanations$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — some decisions are explained; others are not$qb$, $qb$Very low — decisions are frequently made and announced without explanation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C03Q08$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Risk appetite in decision-making in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Well-calibrated — the organisation takes appropriate risks given its strategy and ambitions$qb$, $qb$Generally appropriate — risk appetite is broadly right for the organisation$qb$, $qb$Too cautious — excessive risk aversion is preventing good strategic decisions$qb$, $qb$Too aggressive — the organisation takes on too much risk without adequate assessment$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C03Q09$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Consensus-seeking vs decisive decision-making in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Balanced — the organisation seeks sufficient input before making clear, timely decisions$qb$, $qb$Generally balanced — decisions are broadly inclusive and timely$qb$, $qb$Too consensus-focused — the need for universal agreement slows or prevents decisions$qb$, $qb$Too top-down — decisions are made quickly by leaders without adequate input from those who know most$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C03Q10$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Overall, the decision-making culture in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is a genuine competitive advantage — fast, good decisions accelerate performance$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — decisions are made effectively enough$qb$, $qb$Has notable gaps — decision-making quality or speed problems are limiting the organisation$qb$, $qb$Is a serious concern — decision-making culture problems are significantly harming performance$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C04Q01$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The unwritten rules in your organisation (what really determines how decisions are made and how careers advance) are:$qb$, $qb$Aligned with the stated values — what actually works matches what the organisation says it values$qb$, $qb$Mostly aligned — some divergence exists but it is not significant$qb$, $qb$Partially misaligned — some unwritten rules contradict the stated culture in ways that undermine it$qb$, $qb$Significantly misaligned — the unwritten rules directly contradict the stated culture creating deep cynicism$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C04Q02$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Fear of failure in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Low and constructive — risk-taking is encouraged and failure is treated as learning$qb$, $qb$Moderate — some fear of failure exists but it does not prevent reasonable risk-taking$qb$, $qb$High — fear of failure is a pervasive experience that inhibits initiative and experimentation$qb$, $qb$Very high — fear of failure is one of the most dominant cultural experiences in the organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C04Q03$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Fear of conflict or difficult conversations in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Low — difficult conversations are handled directly and professionally$qb$, $qb$Moderate — most people can handle conflict adequately$qb$, $qb$High — avoidance of conflict is common and this leads to unresolved issues festering$qb$, $qb$Very high — conflict avoidance is so strong that important issues are systematically not discussed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C04Q04$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Fear of being seen as not performing (looking busy vs being productive) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Absent — the culture values outcomes and people are trusted to manage their own work$qb$, $qb$Low — some presenteeism exists but outcomes are valued over activity$qb$, $qb$Moderate — performance theatre (looking busy) is a known cultural feature$qb$, $qb$High — a significant proportion of employee effort goes into managing perceptions rather than producing results$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C04Q05$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Fear of being associated with a failing project or decision in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Low — failure is owned collectively and lessons are shared without blame$qb$, $qb$Moderate — some blame culture exists but it is not dominant$qb$, $qb$High — people actively distance themselves from failing initiatives to avoid career damage$qb$, $qb$Very high — fear of being blamed is so strong that people refuse to take ownership of risky projects$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C04Q06$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Imposter syndrome or fear of inadequacy among employees in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Rare — the culture is supportive and people feel valued for what they contribute$qb$, $qb$Present for some — some individuals experience self-doubt but it is not pervasive$qb$, $qb$Common — many employees do not feel confident that they deserve their roles$qb$, $qb$Very common — a culture of high pressure and limited positive reinforcement creates widespread inadequacy feelings$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C04Q07$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Fear of the boss or authority figures in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Absent — leaders are respected but not feared; the hierarchy does not create anxiety$qb$, $qb$Low — most leaders are approachable and relationships are constructive$qb$, $qb$Moderate — some leaders create a climate of anxiety that affects team performance$qb$, $qb$High — fear of certain leaders is a known and significant cultural barrier to performance and honesty$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C04Q08$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The degree to which politics (career protection, faction-building, impression management) drives behaviour in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Low — the culture rewards contribution over politics and political behaviour is discouraged$qb$, $qb$Moderate — some political behaviour exists as in any large organisation$qb$, $qb$High — political behaviour is a significant feature of the culture and consumes meaningful energy$qb$, $qb$Very high — politics is the dominant driver of behaviour and this is seriously harming the organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C04Q09$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The fear of change among employees in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Low — change is embraced as a normal part of working life in the organisation$qb$, $qb$Moderate — some resistance to change exists but it is manageable$qb$, $qb$High — fear of change is a significant barrier to implementing new ideas or ways of working$qb$, $qb$Very high — change anxiety is so strong that the organisation struggles to implement almost any significant shift$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C04Q10$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Overall, the level of fear and hidden cultural dynamics in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is low — the culture is open, honest, and psychologically safe$qb$, $qb$Is manageable — some cultural dysfunction exists but is not dominant$qb$, $qb$Has significant pockets — fear and hidden dynamics are materially constraining specific parts of the organisation$qb$, $qb$Is a serious concern — pervasive cultural fears and unwritten rules are significantly damaging performance$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C05Q01$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The culture of personal accountability in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — people own their commitments, take responsibility for outcomes, and do not blame others$qb$, $qb$Generally present — most people are accountable most of the time$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — some people are highly accountable; others routinely avoid accountability$qb$, $qb$Weak — blame, excuses, and diffused accountability are a dominant cultural experience$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C05Q02$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$When things go wrong in your organisation, the typical response is:$qb$, $qb$Learning orientation — the focus is on what happened, what we learn, and how we prevent recurrence$qb$, $qb$Generally constructive — problems are addressed professionally$qb$, $qb$Mixed — some problems are handled constructively; others devolve into blame$qb$, $qb$Blame — the primary response to failure is finding who is responsible rather than fixing the problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C05Q03$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The follow-through culture in your organisation (whether people do what they say they will do) is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — commitments are taken seriously and delivery is reliable$qb$, $qb$Generally reliable — most people follow through on most commitments$qb$, $qb$Variable — some people have high follow-through; others are consistently unreliable$qb$, $qb$Weak — follow-through is poor across the organisation and this creates significant frustration$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C05Q04$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The quality of ownership for cross-functional outcomes (where no single person is clearly responsible) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — cross-functional accountability is clear and people take ownership beyond their own role$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — most cross-functional outcomes have identifiable owners$qb$, $qb$Weak — cross-functional ownership gaps are a frequent source of things falling through the cracks$qb$, $qb$Very weak — diffused accountability for cross-functional outcomes is a persistent and significant problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C05Q05$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The culture of proactive problem-solving (identifying and resolving problems before being asked) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — employees at all levels proactively surface and solve problems without waiting to be directed$qb$, $qb$Generally present — most employees will address problems when they see them$qb$, $qb$Weak — most employees wait for direction before addressing problems$qb$, $qb$Very weak — a passive culture means problems are not raised or addressed until they become crises$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C05Q06$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Senior leadership accountability modelling in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — senior leaders visibly own their outcomes, acknowledge failures, and model the accountability culture$qb$, $qb$Generally good — most senior leaders model accountability reasonably well$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — some senior leaders model good accountability; others do not$qb$, $qb$Poor — senior leader avoidance of accountability is a significant cultural problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C05Q07$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The degree to which employees take ownership of customer outcomes (rather than just completing tasks) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — employees think and act like owners of the customer experience$qb$, $qb$Generally present — most employees go beyond their defined tasks when customers need it$qb$, $qb$Variable — customer ownership thinking varies significantly across teams$qb$, $qb$Low — most employees focus on completing their tasks without broader ownership of outcomes$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C05Q08$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Consequences for non-delivery of commitments in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Clear and consistently applied — non-delivery has consequences at all levels$qb$, $qb$Generally present — most non-delivery is addressed$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — consequences depend on seniority and relationships$qb$, $qb$Absent — commitments are frequently not delivered without meaningful consequence$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
