  ($qb$S16C10Q04$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Follow-through on culture improvement commitments in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Reliable — when the organisation commits to cultural improvements, they are implemented$qb$, $qb$Generally reliable — most commitments are followed through$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — some culture commitments are fulfilled; others are not$qb$, $qb$Poor — culture improvement commitments are frequently not followed through undermining trust$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C10Q05$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Qualitative culture insight (beyond surveys — listening sessions, focus groups, exit data analysis) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Regularly used — qualitative methods are used alongside surveys for a richer picture$qb$, $qb$Occasionally used — some qualitative methods supplement survey data$qb$, $qb$Rarely used — the organisation relies primarily on survey data without deeper qualitative insight$qb$, $qb$Not used — the organisation does not gather qualitative culture insight$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C10Q06$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Manager accountability for culture within their teams in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Clear — managers are held accountable for the culture within their teams through measurement and review$qb$, $qb$Generally present — managers are broadly accountable$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — accountability for culture varies significantly by manager and level$qb$, $qb$Absent — managers are not held accountable for the culture within their teams$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C10Q07$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Culture change programme quality in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — when culture change is initiated, it is structured, resourced, and sustained$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — culture change programmes are reasonably designed$qb$, $qb$Variable — culture change quality varies significantly between initiatives$qb$, $qb$Poor — culture change programmes are typically under-resourced and fail to achieve their objectives$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C10Q08$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The use of external benchmarks to understand the quality of the culture in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Regular — the organisation benchmarks its culture data against industry peers$qb$, $qb$Occasional — benchmarking occurs periodically$qb$, $qb$Rare — the organisation rarely benchmarks its culture externally$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation does not benchmark its culture against external data$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C10Q09$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The integration of culture health into leadership and executive decision-making in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — culture data is a regular input into leadership decisions$qb$, $qb$Generally present — culture is considered in major leadership decisions$qb$, $qb$Limited — culture is considered reactively when a specific problem forces it$qb$, $qb$Absent — culture health is not systematically integrated into leadership decision-making$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S16C10Q10$qb$, $qb$S16$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Overall, the quality of culture measurement and improvement in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is a genuine capability — the organisation understands its culture and is systematically improving it$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — the organisation measures culture and acts on the findings$qb$, $qb$Has notable gaps — culture measurement and improvement is not robust enough$qb$, $qb$Is insufficient — the organisation has poor insight into its culture and is not effectively improving it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true);
