  ($qb$S17C01Q01$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Senior leadership's awareness of major industry trends affecting the organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Deep and current — leadership actively tracks and discusses emerging trends and their strategic implications$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — leadership is broadly aware of major trends$qb$, $qb$Limited — leadership is aware of some trends but not comprehensively engaged with the full landscape$qb$, $qb$Very limited — leadership has poor visibility of trends shaping their industry$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C01Q02$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's process for monitoring industry trends in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Systematic — structured intelligence gathering and regular reporting to leadership is in place$qb$, $qb$Adequate — key sources of trend information are monitored$qb$, $qb$Ad hoc — trend monitoring depends on individual initiative without a structured process$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — there is no systematic approach to tracking industry trends$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C01Q03$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The breadth of trends being tracked by the organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Wide — technology, regulatory, demographic, economic, and market trends are all monitored$qb$, $qb$Broad — most major trend categories are covered$qb$, $qb$Narrow — primarily near-term competitive and market trends are tracked$qb$, $qb$Very narrow — only immediate competitive threats are monitored$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C01Q04$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The use of external advisors, analysts, or trend experts in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Regular — relevant external expertise is used to supplement internal trend analysis$qb$, $qb$Occasional — external perspectives are sought periodically$qb$, $qb$Rare — external trend expertise is only brought in for specific strategic decisions$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation relies entirely on internal knowledge for trend analysis$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C01Q05$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Scenario planning based on industry trend developments in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Structured — multiple strategic scenarios based on trend trajectories are regularly developed$qb$, $qb$Periodic — scenario planning occurs during major planning cycles$qb$, $qb$Occasional — some scenario thinking happens without formal structure$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — strategic planning does not incorporate formal scenario analysis$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C01Q06$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The time horizon of trend monitoring in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Long (5-10 year horizon) — the organisation proactively tracks long-term structural changes$qb$, $qb$Medium (2-5 year horizon) — medium-term trends are monitored$qb$, $qb$Short (0-2 year horizon) — trend monitoring focuses primarily on near-term developments$qb$, $qb$Very short — the organisation focuses almost exclusively on immediate competitive developments$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C01Q07$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Cross-industry trend borrowing (learning from trends in adjacent or completely different industries) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Active — the organisation regularly draws lessons from trends in other industries$qb$, $qb$Occasional — some cross-industry learning occurs$qb$, $qb$Rare — the organisation primarily focuses on trends within its own sector$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — there is no structured exposure to trends from other industries$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C01Q08$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The quality of intelligence about competitor and adjacent market player strategic moves is:$qb$, $qb$High — competitor intelligence is systematically gathered and regularly reported$qb$, $qb$Adequate — major competitor moves are tracked$qb$, $qb$Limited — competitor intelligence is primarily gathered through sales encounters$qb$, $qb$Very limited — competitor strategic intelligence is poor$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C01Q09$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+E$qb$, $qb$The diversity of voices in the trend-monitoring process (from different levels and functions) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — frontline, customer-facing, and specialist insights are all incorporated into trend analysis$qb$, $qb$Moderate — some diversity of perspective is included$qb$, $qb$Limited — trend analysis is primarily a senior leadership activity$qb$, $qb$Very limited — a small group of leaders determines what trends are relevant$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C01Q10$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Overall, industry trend awareness and monitoring in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is a genuine competitive advantage — the organisation sees changes coming and prepares in advance$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — major trends are identified with reasonable timeliness$qb$, $qb$Has notable gaps — the organisation is sometimes surprised by industry changes it should have anticipated$qb$, $qb$Is insufficient — poor trend monitoring regularly leaves the organisation unprepared for change$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C02Q01$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's ability to change its strategy in response to significant external developments is:$qb$, $qb$High — the organisation can pivot quickly when the environment requires it$qb$, $qb$Adequate — major strategic shifts can be made though not always with sufficient speed$qb$, $qb$Limited — strategic inertia means the organisation is slow to change direction$qb$, $qb$Very limited — the organisation is trapped by its current strategy and struggles to adapt$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C02Q02$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The frequency of strategic review in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Continuous — the strategy is a living document reviewed at least quarterly against external developments$qb$, $qb$Annual — strategy is formally reviewed once a year$qb$, $qb$Irregular — strategic review happens when a significant event forces it$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the strategy is set and not meaningfully reviewed or updated$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C02Q03$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's track record of successfully navigating major industry disruptions in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — the organisation has consistently adapted well to significant disruptions$qb$, $qb$Generally positive — major disruptions have been navigated with reasonable effectiveness$qb$, $qb$Mixed — some disruptions have been navigated well; others have caused significant problems$qb$, $qb$Poor — the organisation has historically struggled to adapt to major disruptions$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C02Q04$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The speed of decision-making in response to significant external changes in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Fast — the organisation can make and implement major strategic decisions within weeks$qb$, $qb$Moderate — significant decisions are made within a quarter$qb$, $qb$Slow — major strategic decisions typically take many months$qb$, $qb$Very slow — the organisation takes over a year to make and implement significant strategic responses$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C02Q05$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Experimentation and pilot programmes to test future strategic options in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Active — the organisation regularly pilots new approaches before committing to full-scale change$qb$, $qb$Occasional — some pilots occur but not as a systematic practice$qb$, $qb$Rare — the organisation prefers to make full commitment decisions rather than pilot first$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation does not use pilot programmes to test future directions$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C02Q06$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The organisation's culture of learning from external case studies and competitor examples is:$qb$, $qb$Active — examples from other organisations are regularly used to inform strategy and practice$qb$, $qb$Present — some learning from external examples occurs$qb$, $qb$Limited — the organisation primarily focuses internally for learning$qb$, $qb$Very limited — the organisation rarely looks externally for strategic learning$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C02Q07$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The board's engagement with strategic agility and the organisation's ability to adapt to change is:$qb$, $qb$Active — the board regularly challenges the management team on strategic preparedness$qb$, $qb$Adequate — the board engages with strategic risk and adaptability periodically$qb$, $qb$Limited — the board primarily focuses on financial performance without deep engagement with strategic agility$qb$, $qb$Minimal — the board does not meaningfully engage with the organisation's future readiness$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C02Q08$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's portfolio of strategic options (what it could do next) is:$qb$, $qb$Rich — the organisation has multiple credible strategic options and actively develops new ones$qb$, $qb$Adequate — some strategic options are identified$qb$, $qb$Limited — the organisation has a narrow view of its strategic options$qb$, $qb$Very narrow — the organisation has very few identified strategic options beyond its current path$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C02Q09$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Organisational resilience (the ability to bounce back from significant adversity) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — the organisation has a demonstrated track record of recovering quickly from setbacks$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — the organisation recovers from setbacks with reasonable speed$qb$, $qb$Limited — significant setbacks cause extended periods of underperformance$qb$, $qb$Very limited — the organisation struggles to recover effectively from major adversity$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C02Q10$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Overall, strategic agility and adaptive capacity in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is a genuine competitive advantage — the organisation adapts faster and better than competitors$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — the organisation manages strategic change effectively$qb$, $qb$Has notable gaps — strategic inertia is slowing the organisation's response to environmental change$qb$, $qb$Is a serious concern — poor strategic agility is creating growing competitive vulnerability$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C03Q01$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's understanding of how workforce requirements will change over the next 5 years is:$qb$, $qb$Deep — workforce planning explicitly incorporates skill evolution, demographic shifts, and automation impact$qb$, $qb$Adequate — major workforce changes are anticipated in strategic planning$qb$, $qb$Limited — workforce future planning focuses on near-term gaps without long-term structural analysis$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation does not plan for future workforce requirements$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C03Q02$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+E$qb$, $qb$Investment in reskilling and future-proofing the workforce in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strategic and funded — significant investment is made in developing skills required for the future$qb$, $qb$Growing — investment is increasing but is not yet at the scale required$qb$, $qb$Limited — some reskilling occurs but it is insufficient for the scale of change coming$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation does not invest in reskilling for future workforce requirements$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C03Q03$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Digital skill readiness of the workforce in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — the workforce is digitally capable and adapts quickly to new tools and ways of working$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — the workforce manages digital tools acceptably$qb$, $qb$Variable — digital skill levels vary widely across the workforce$qb$, $qb$Low — significant digital skill gaps are a known challenge and are not being adequately addressed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C03Q04$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+E$qb$, $qb$AI literacy and readiness across the organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — employees understand AI and are prepared to work effectively alongside AI tools$qb$, $qb$Developing — AI literacy is being built through structured programmes$qb$, $qb$Limited — AI literacy is low and unstructured$qb$, $qb$Very limited — the organisation has not begun building AI literacy in its workforce$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C03Q05$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's talent strategy explicitly addresses roles that will change significantly due to technology is:$qb$, $qb$Yes — specific plans exist for roles most affected by automation and AI$qb$, $qb$Partially — some attention is given to technology-impacted roles$qb$, $qb$Minimally — the impact of technology on specific roles is acknowledged but not planned for$qb$, $qb$No — the talent strategy does not explicitly address technology-driven role changes$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C03Q06$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Attraction of Gen Z and millennial talent in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — the organisation is seen as an attractive employer by younger talent$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — the organisation can attract young talent$qb$, $qb$Challenging — the organisation is not seen as an attractive employer by younger talent$qb$, $qb$Very challenging — the organisation significantly struggles to attract younger generations$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C03Q07$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Intergenerational knowledge transfer (ensuring critical knowledge moves from experienced to newer employees) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Structured and proactive — knowledge transfer programmes are in place and working$qb$, $qb$Generally managed — some knowledge transfer processes exist$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — knowledge transfer happens informally in some areas but not systematically$qb$, $qb$Poor — significant institutional knowledge is at risk as experienced employees retire or leave$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C03Q08$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Flexible and alternative work model readiness in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — the organisation has successfully implemented flexible and hybrid working at scale$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — flexible working is in place and broadly effective$qb$, $qb$Partially developed — flexible working exists but with operational gaps$qb$, $qb$Underdeveloped — the organisation has struggled to implement effective flexible and alternative work models$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C03Q09$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Employee adaptability and continuous learning mindset in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — employees embrace continuous learning and see adaptability as a core professional skill$qb$, $qb$Generally positive — most employees are open to learning and change$qb$, $qb$Variable — learning mindset varies significantly across the workforce$qb$, $qb$Low — many employees are resistant to learning new skills or changing how they work$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C03Q10$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Overall, workforce future readiness in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is a genuine competitive strength — the workforce is well-prepared for the changes ahead$qb$, $qb$Is developing — meaningful workforce future readiness investment is underway$qb$, $qb$Has notable gaps — the workforce is not adequately prepared for the near-term changes coming$qb$, $qb$Is a serious concern — workforce future readiness gaps represent a significant strategic risk$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C04Q01$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The integration of sustainability into business strategy in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Deep — sustainability is a core element of the business strategy with clear goals and investment$qb$, $qb$Developing — sustainability is becoming a strategic priority$qb$, $qb$Limited — sustainability is addressed mainly through compliance without strategic integration$qb$, $qb$Absent — sustainability is not part of the business strategy$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C04Q02$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's net zero or carbon reduction commitments in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Defined, funded, and on track — clear commitments exist and the organisation is meeting them$qb$, $qb$Defined — commitments exist but implementation is in early stages$qb$, $qb$Partly defined — some carbon reduction goals exist without comprehensive commitments$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation has not made carbon reduction commitments$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C04Q03$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$ESG as a competitive differentiator in your market in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Well-established — ESG credentials are a recognised source of competitive advantage$qb$, $qb$Developing — ESG positioning is growing in importance for the organisation$qb$, $qb$Beginning — the organisation is starting to use ESG as a differentiator$qb$, $qb$Not used — the organisation does not position ESG as a competitive differentiator$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C04Q04$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's approach to supply chain sustainability in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Proactive — sustainability standards are set for suppliers and compliance is monitored$qb$, $qb$Developing — some supplier sustainability requirements are in place$qb$, $qb$Limited — supplier sustainability is addressed only for major regulatory requirements$qb$, $qb$Absent — supply chain sustainability is not part of the organisation's sustainability programme$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C04Q05$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The quality and transparency of ESG reporting in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Comprehensive and externally verified — the organisation produces credible, detailed ESG reports$qb$, $qb$Adequate — regular ESG reporting is produced$qb$, $qb$Basic — limited ESG reporting exists$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation does not produce sustainability reporting$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C04Q06$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The board's engagement with ESG and sustainability in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Deep and active — the board has specific sustainability expertise and oversight responsibility$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — ESG is a regular board agenda item$qb$, $qb$Periodic — ESG is reviewed by the board annually$qb$, $qb$Limited — ESG receives minimal board attention$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C04Q07$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Employee engagement with the sustainability agenda in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — employees are energised by the sustainability agenda and actively contribute to it$qb$, $qb$Generally positive — most employees support the sustainability agenda$qb$, $qb$Mixed — some employees are engaged; others are indifferent$qb$, $qb$Low — employees do not see sustainability as relevant to their work$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C04Q08$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+E$qb$, $qb$The organisation's use of sustainability as a talent attraction tool in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Active — sustainability credentials are a deliberate part of the employer value proposition$qb$, $qb$Developing — sustainability is increasingly used in employer branding$qb$, $qb$Limited — sustainability is mentioned but is not a meaningful talent attraction tool$qb$, $qb$Not used — sustainability is not part of the employer brand$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C04Q09$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Customer demand for sustainable products or services in your market and how the organisation is responding is:$qb$, $qb$Well-met — the organisation's sustainability offerings match or lead customer demand$qb$, $qb$Being developed — sustainable offerings are in development$qb$, $qb$Partially addressed — some sustainable offerings exist but they do not fully meet market demand$qb$, $qb$Not addressed — the organisation has not responded to customer sustainability demand$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C04Q10$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Overall, sustainability and ESG strategy in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is a genuine competitive advantage — the organisation leads peers on sustainability$qb$, $qb$Is developing — meaningful sustainability progress is underway$qb$, $qb$Is in early stages — sustainability strategy needs significant development$qb$, $qb$Is a competitive liability — the organisation is significantly behind peers on sustainability$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C05Q01$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's assessment of technology disruption risk to its business model is:$qb$, $qb$Comprehensive — technology disruption scenarios are regularly assessed and business model resilience is tested$qb$, $qb$Adequate — major technology disruption risks are assessed$qb$, $qb$Limited — technology disruption risk is acknowledged but not comprehensively assessed$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation has not formally assessed technology disruption risk to its business model$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
