  ($qb$S17C10Q01$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's overall future readiness (across technology, talent, culture, market, and financial dimensions) is:$qb$, $qb$High — the organisation is comprehensively prepared for the next 3-5 years$qb$, $qb$Developing — meaningful future readiness work is underway across most dimensions$qb$, $qb$Uneven — some dimensions are well-prepared; others have significant gaps$qb$, $qb$Low — the organisation is broadly underprepared for the changes ahead$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C10Q02$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The executive team's collective conviction that the organisation needs to change significantly to remain competitive is:$qb$, $qb$Strong and shared — all leaders understand the scale of change required and are committed to it$qb$, $qb$Generally present — most leaders recognise the need for change$qb$, $qb$Mixed — some leaders are convinced; others are complacent about the future$qb$, $qb$Weak — leadership complacency is a significant barrier to necessary transformation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C10Q03$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's investment in building future readiness as an explicit strategic priority is:$qb$, $qb$High — future readiness is a funded strategic programme with defined outcomes and accountability$qb$, $qb$Growing — investment in future readiness is increasing$qb$, $qb$Limited — some future readiness investment exists but it is not comprehensive or sustained$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — future readiness is not an explicit strategic priority or investment$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C10Q04$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Employee belief in the organisation's future relevance and competitive viability is:$qb$, $qb$High — employees are confident the organisation will remain competitive and relevant$qb$, $qb$Generally present — most employees believe in the organisation's future$qb$, $qb$Mixed — some employees are confident; others are uncertain or pessimistic$qb$, $qb$Low — employee confidence in the organisation's future is weak$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C10Q05$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+E$qb$, $qb$The quality of the organisation's strategic narrative about its future in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Compelling and credible — the future story is clear, inspiring, and grounded in real capability$qb$, $qb$Generally clear — the future narrative is defined though not always compelling$qb$, $qb$Vague — the future narrative lacks clarity or credibility$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation does not have a credible narrative about its future$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C10Q06$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's transformation velocity (the pace at which it is changing relative to the pace required) is:$qb$, $qb$Sufficient — transformation is happening fast enough to keep pace with the environment$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — transformation is progressing though pace could be faster$qb$, $qb$Insufficient — transformation pace is lagging the rate of change in the environment$qb$, $qb$Significantly insufficient — the gap between transformation speed and environmental change is growing$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C10Q07$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The degree to which future readiness is embedded in day-to-day management rather than being a separate programme is:$qb$, $qb$High — future readiness thinking is integrated into how the organisation manages itself day to day$qb$, $qb$Developing — some integration of future readiness into regular management practice$qb$, $qb$Limited — future readiness is primarily a separate strategic initiative not embedded in management$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — future readiness is addressed only in occasional strategy off-sites$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C10Q08$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The quality of future readiness metrics and tracking in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — the organisation tracks leading indicators of future readiness and reviews progress regularly$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — some future readiness metrics are tracked$qb$, $qb$Limited — future readiness is discussed but not robustly measured$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation has no systematic measurement of its future readiness$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C10Q09$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Leadership development for future-oriented leadership skills (digital, agile, complexity management) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Comprehensive — leadership development is explicitly preparing leaders for future demands$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — some future-oriented leadership development is in place$qb$, $qb$Limited — leadership development is primarily focused on current skill needs$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — leadership development does not address future-oriented leadership requirements$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S17C10Q10$qb$, $qb$S17$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Overall, when you assess the organisation's total future readiness and transformation capacity:$qb$, $qb$The organisation is well-positioned to thrive in its future competitive environment$qb$, $qb$The organisation is developing meaningfully and is on track for future competitiveness$qb$, $qb$The organisation has significant gaps that need urgent attention to remain competitive$qb$, $qb$The organisation is significantly underprepared and faces serious strategic risk if the pace of change does not accelerate$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true);
INSERT INTO question_bank (question_id, section_code, category_code, level_tag, text, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, score_a, score_b, score_c, score_d, active) VALUES
  ($qb$S18C01Q01$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Employees in your organisation feel their work is meaningful:$qb$, $qb$Yes — the vast majority feel genuine meaning and purpose in their daily work$qb$, $qb$Generally — most employees feel their work is reasonably meaningful$qb$, $qb$Partly — some employees feel meaning; others feel their work is just a job$qb$, $qb$Rarely — most employees do not feel their work is meaningful$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C01Q02$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The connection between individual roles and the organisation's purpose in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Clear and compelling — every employee can see how their work contributes to something larger$qb$, $qb$Generally clear — most employees understand how their role connects to the organisation's goals$qb$, $qb$Weak — the connection is stated but not felt in day-to-day work$qb$, $qb$Absent — employees cannot connect their work to any broader purpose$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C01Q03$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation communicates the social or customer impact of its work to employees:$qb$, $qb$Regularly and powerfully — impact stories are shared in a way that creates genuine pride and motivation$qb$, $qb$Occasionally — some communication of impact occurs$qb$, $qb$Rarely — the organisation seldom shares stories of impact with employees$qb$, $qb$Never — employees receive no information about the difference their work makes$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C01Q04$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The degree to which employees feel proud of the work they do in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — employees are genuinely proud of the quality of their work and the organisation's contribution$qb$, $qb$Generally present — most employees feel a reasonable degree of pride$qb$, $qb$Variable — some employees feel proud; others feel indifferent$qb$, $qb$Low — most employees do not feel proud of their work or the organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C01Q05$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The alignment between organisational values and employee personal values in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — most employees feel the organisation's values align with their own$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — there is sufficient values alignment to create engagement$qb$, $qb$Weak — some misalignment between organisational and personal values creates friction$qb$, $qb$Very weak — widespread values misalignment is a significant engagement and retention problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C01Q06$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The sense of community and belonging among employees in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — employees feel a genuine sense of belonging and community at work$qb$, $qb$Generally present — most employees feel reasonably connected$qb$, $qb$Variable — some teams have strong community; others feel isolated$qb$, $qb$Weak — many employees do not feel a sense of belonging or community$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C01Q07$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The experience of contributing to something bigger than oneself in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strongly felt — employees regularly experience the sense of being part of something meaningful$qb$, $qb$Sometimes felt — the experience occurs for some employees in some contexts$qb$, $qb$Rarely felt — the organisation has not created conditions for this experience$qb$, $qb$Not felt — employees do not experience a sense of contributing to anything beyond their immediate tasks$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C01Q08$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Post-work sense of fulfilment (employees feeling satisfied at the end of the day) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — most employees end the day feeling a sense of accomplishment$qb$, $qb$Generally present — most employees feel reasonably fulfilled in their work$qb$, $qb$Variable — some employees feel fulfilled; others regularly feel their effort was wasted$qb$, $qb$Low — most employees do not experience work-related fulfilment$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C01Q09$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Career meaning (whether the work the organisation provides constitutes a meaningful career path) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — the organisation provides a genuinely meaningful career path that attracts and retains$qb$, $qb$Adequate — careers at the organisation are meaningful to most employees$qb$, $qb$Weak — many employees do not see a meaningful career path within the organisation$qb$, $qb$Very weak — the organisation struggles to retain people because the career is not seen as meaningful$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C01Q10$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Overall, the sense of purpose and meaning in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is a genuine competitive advantage for talent attraction and retention$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — employees are sufficiently motivated by the work$qb$, $qb$Has notable gaps — insufficient purpose and meaning is contributing to disengagement$qb$, $qb$Is a serious concern — absence of purpose and meaning is a primary driver of disengagement and attrition$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C02Q01$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Managers in your organisation understand what motivates each person on their team:$qb$, $qb$Yes — most managers actively learn what motivates individuals and adapt their approach$qb$, $qb$Generally — most managers have adequate awareness of their team's motivational drivers$qb$, $qb$Inconsistently — some managers are attuned to individual motivation; others apply a one-size approach$qb$, $qb$Rarely — managers do not generally consider individual motivational differences$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C02Q02$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Managers in your organisation take regular, genuine interest in their team members as people:$qb$, $qb$Yes — regular, caring one-to-one conversations are a cultural norm$qb$, $qb$Generally — most managers show reasonable care for their team members$qb$, $qb$Inconsistently — quality of manager care varies significantly$qb$, $qb$Rarely — most managers are primarily focused on tasks and results without genuine personal interest$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C02Q03$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Managers in your organisation give timely, specific recognition for good work:$qb$, $qb$Consistently — good work is recognised promptly and specifically by most managers$qb$, $qb$Generally — recognition happens for significant achievements$qb$, $qb$Inconsistently — recognition practices vary significantly between managers$qb$, $qb$Rarely — most employees do not receive regular recognition from their manager$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C02Q04$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Managers in your organisation support employee career development actively:$qb$, $qb$Yes — most managers are actively invested in developing their team members' careers$qb$, $qb$Generally — managers provide reasonable development support$qb$, $qb$Inconsistently — some managers are great developers; others show little interest in employee growth$qb$, $qb$Rarely — manager-led career development is not a cultural norm$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C02Q05$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Managers in your organisation protect their teams from unnecessary bureaucracy and obstacles:$qb$, $qb$Yes — managers actively remove friction that prevents their team from doing great work$qb$, $qb$Generally — managers provide reasonable protection from unnecessary bureaucracy$qb$, $qb$Inconsistently — some managers advocate for their teams; others allow obstacles to accumulate$qb$, $qb$Rarely — employees rarely feel their manager is protecting them from the organisation's friction$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C02Q06$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Employees in your organisation feel their manager trusts them to do their job well:$qb$, $qb$Yes — employee autonomy and manager trust are a defining feature of the culture$qb$, $qb$Generally — most employees feel trusted by their manager$qb$, $qb$Variably — trust varies significantly by manager and team$qb$, $qb$No — most employees feel micromanaged or insufficiently trusted$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C02Q07$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The quality of the manager's communication (clarity, transparency, regularity) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — managers communicate clearly, regularly, and transparently with their teams$qb$, $qb$Generally good — most managers communicate adequately$qb$, $qb$Variable — communication quality varies significantly between managers$qb$, $qb$Poor — most managers are poor communicators and this is a significant source of team frustration$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C02Q08$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Managers in your organisation are accessible and approachable to their team members:$qb$, $qb$Yes — managers are genuinely accessible and employees feel comfortable approaching them$qb$, $qb$Generally — most managers are reasonably accessible$qb$, $qb$Inconsistently — some managers are very accessible; others are difficult to reach or unapproachable$qb$, $qb$Rarely — most managers are not accessible enough and this creates frustration$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C02Q09$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Managers in your organisation inspire their teams toward high performance:$qb$, $qb$Yes — most managers create a motivating environment that draws out the best in people$qb$, $qb$Generally — managers motivate adequately$qb$, $qb$Inconsistently — some managers inspire; others demotivate or leave teams unmoved$qb$, $qb$Rarely — most managers do not create an inspiring performance environment$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C02Q10$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Overall, manager-led motivation in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is a genuine competitive advantage — most managers are excellent motivators$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — managers motivate their teams well enough$qb$, $qb$Has notable gaps — inconsistent manager quality is creating uneven motivation across the organisation$qb$, $qb$Is a serious concern — poor manager-led motivation is a primary driver of disengagement and attrition$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C03Q01$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The frequency of genuine recognition of employee contribution in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — recognition is a regular and natural part of how the organisation operates$qb$, $qb$Adequate — significant achievements are recognised$qb$, $qb$Low — recognition is infrequent and most employees feel underappreciated$qb$, $qb$Very low — recognition is rare and most employees feel their contribution is invisible$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C03Q02$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The specificity and sincerity of recognition in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — recognition is specific, personal, and genuine$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — recognition is reasonably sincere$qb$, $qb$Variable — some recognition feels genuine; other recognition feels performative$qb$, $qb$Low — recognition in this organisation often feels generic or insincere$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C03Q03$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Recognition that is visible to peers (public recognition of achievement) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$A regular feature — peer visibility of recognition amplifies its motivational impact$qb$, $qb$Occasional — some public recognition happens$qb$, $qb$Rare — most recognition is private and informal$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation does not use visible recognition mechanisms$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C03Q04$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Non-monetary recognition (heartfelt thank-yous, awards, special opportunities) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Well-developed and genuinely valued$qb$, $qb$Present — some non-monetary recognition exists$qb$, $qb$Minimal — the organisation relies primarily on financial rewards without much non-monetary recognition$qb$, $qb$Absent — non-monetary recognition does not meaningfully exist$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C03Q05$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Celebration of team and collective achievement in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Regular and genuine — team wins are celebrated in ways that build camaraderie$qb$, $qb$Occasional — major team achievements are celebrated$qb$, $qb$Rare — team achievement is seldom recognised or celebrated$qb$, $qb$Absent — collective achievement goes unrecognised$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C03Q06$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Cross-functional recognition (recognising contributions that benefit the wider organisation beyond one's team) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Active — leaders regularly recognise contributions that extend beyond departmental boundaries$qb$, $qb$Occasional — some cross-functional recognition occurs$qb$, $qb$Rare — recognition is primarily confined to direct team and manager relationships$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — cross-functional contributions are rarely recognised$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C03Q07$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Peer recognition programmes or mechanisms in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Well-established and actively used — peer recognition is a meaningful part of the recognition culture$qb$, $qb$Present — some peer recognition mechanisms exist$qb$, $qb$Limited — peer recognition is informal and infrequent$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — there are no structured peer recognition mechanisms$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C03Q08$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The speed of recognition (how quickly good work is recognised) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Fast — good work is recognised within days of occurring$qb$, $qb$Generally timely — recognition usually happens within weeks$qb$, $qb$Slow — recognition often happens so long after the event that its motivational impact is reduced$qb$, $qb$Very slow or absent — recognition frequently does not occur or arrives so late it loses meaning$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C03Q09$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Employee perception of whether their contribution is valued in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — most employees feel genuinely valued for what they contribute$qb$, $qb$Generally present — employees feel reasonably valued$qb$, $qb$Variable — some feel valued; others do not$qb$, $qb$Low — most employees do not feel their contributions are valued by the organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C03Q10$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Overall, the recognition and appreciation culture in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is a genuine competitive advantage — exceptional recognition drives exceptional effort$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — employees feel sufficiently appreciated$qb$, $qb$Has notable gaps — underappreciation is a persistent source of disengagement$qb$, $qb$Is a serious concern — the absence of recognition is one of the most significant engagement and retention problems$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C04Q01$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The degree of autonomy employees have in how they approach their work in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — employees have meaningful freedom in how they accomplish their goals$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — reasonable autonomy exists$qb$, $qb$Limited — most employees are closely directed with limited autonomy$qb$, $qb$Very limited — micromanagement is pervasive and autonomy is minimal$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C04Q02$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The opportunities for employees to develop mastery (becoming genuinely excellent at their craft) in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — the organisation provides rich opportunities for deep skill development$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — reasonable development opportunities are available$qb$, $qb$Limited — opportunities for genuine skill mastery are constrained$qb$, $qb$Very limited — employees cannot meaningfully develop expertise in their field through their work$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C04Q03$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The degree to which employees are regularly given stretching, growth-oriented work in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — most employees regularly work on challenges that develop and stretch them$qb$, $qb$Generally present — some stretch opportunities exist$qb$, $qb$Limited — most work is routine without significant growth opportunity$qb$, $qb$Very limited — employees are rarely given work that challenges them to grow$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C04Q04$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The quality of formal learning and development opportunities in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — learning opportunities are comprehensive, relevant, and actively used$qb$, $qb$Good — meaningful development opportunities are available$qb$, $qb$Adequate — some development is provided but not with sufficient depth or relevance$qb$, $qb$Poor — formal learning and development opportunities are minimal or irrelevant$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C04Q05$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The investment in leadership and management development in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Significant — leadership development is a genuine strategic investment producing measurable leaders$qb$, $qb$Adequate — some leadership development investment is in place$qb$, $qb$Limited — leadership development is insufficient for the organisation's needs$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation does not invest in leadership development$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C04Q06$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Employees' ability to bring their full skills and capabilities to their role in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — most employees feel their talents are fully utilised$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — most employees' main skills are used$qb$, $qb$Partly — some employees feel their talents are underutilised$qb$, $qb$Very limited — widespread talent underutilisation is a significant engagement problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C04Q07$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The quality of mentoring and coaching available to employees in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — employees have access to great mentoring and coaching that drives growth$qb$, $qb$Good — meaningful mentoring and coaching is available$qb$, $qb$Limited — mentoring and coaching is available to only some employees$qb$, $qb$Very limited — mentoring and coaching are not meaningfully available$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
