  ($qb$S18C09Q04$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$360-degree feedback or peer feedback practices in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Well-established and valued — multi-directional feedback is embedded in the development culture$qb$, $qb$Present — some 360 or peer feedback practice exists$qb$, $qb$Limited — multi-directional feedback is used only in specific contexts$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — feedback flows only downward from managers to employees$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C09Q05$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The quality of formal performance review processes in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — performance reviews are well-designed, well-facilitated, and produce genuine development insight$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — performance reviews serve a useful purpose$qb$, $qb$Variable — quality of performance review conversations varies significantly$qb$, $qb$Poor — performance reviews are widely experienced as formulaic, stressful, or useless$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C09Q06$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The connection between performance feedback and reward in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Clear — feedback directly informs reward decisions in a transparent and credible way$qb$, $qb$Generally present — feedback influences reward though the connection is not always transparent$qb$, $qb$Weak — feedback and reward decisions are often disconnected$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — there is no clear connection between performance feedback and reward outcomes$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C09Q07$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The culture of continuous improvement in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — employees at all levels are always looking for better ways to work$qb$, $qb$Generally present — continuous improvement is a valued cultural attribute$qb$, $qb$Patchy — some teams continuously improve; others maintain the status quo$qb$, $qb$Weak — the culture is comfortable with the status quo and does not value continuous improvement$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C09Q08$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The psychological safety to give upward feedback (employees giving feedback to their managers) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — upward feedback is actively sought and safely given$qb$, $qb$Generally present — most employees feel they can give reasonable feedback upward$qb$, $qb$Low — most employees are cautious about giving feedback to their managers$qb$, $qb$Very low — giving feedback to a manager carries significant perceived risk$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C09Q09$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The degree to which feedback leads to visible behaviour change in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — feedback is demonstrably acted on and this reinforces the feedback culture$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — feedback produces some behaviour change$qb$, $qb$Limited — feedback is received but seldom produces visible change$qb$, $qb$Very limited — feedback does not lead to change and this is demotivating for those giving it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C09Q10$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Overall, the feedback culture and continuous improvement orientation in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is a genuine competitive advantage — the organisation learns and improves faster than peers$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — feedback and improvement culture are reasonably strong$qb$, $qb$Has notable gaps — feedback culture weaknesses are limiting development and performance$qb$, $qb$Is a serious concern — poor feedback culture is significantly constraining learning and performance$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C10Q01$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The primary drivers of employee motivation in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Well-understood — leadership has a nuanced, data-driven understanding of what motivates employees$qb$, $qb$Generally understood — the main motivational drivers are known$qb$, $qb$Partially understood — some motivational drivers are known but the picture is incomplete$qb$, $qb$Not understood — the organisation does not have a clear picture of what motivates its employees$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C10Q02$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The alignment between what the organisation offers and what motivates its employees in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — the employee experience is well-designed around genuine motivational drivers$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — the main motivational needs are met$qb$, $qb$Partial — some motivational needs are well-met; others are not adequately addressed$qb$, $qb$Weak — significant misalignment between what the organisation offers and what motivates employees$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C10Q03$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The ability of the organisation to tailor the employee experience to individual motivational needs in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — managers actively personalise the work experience for individuals$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — some personalisation of the employee experience occurs$qb$, $qb$Limited — the employee experience is largely standardised without meaningful personalisation$qb$, $qb$Very limited — the organisation offers the same experience to everyone regardless of individual needs$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C10Q04$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The degree to which employees feel the organisation genuinely cares about their wellbeing and career in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — most employees feel genuinely cared for as people, not just as resources$qb$, $qb$Generally present — the organisation demonstrates adequate care for employees$qb$, $qb$Variable — some employees feel genuinely cared for; others do not$qb$, $qb$Low — most employees feel the organisation treats them primarily as a resource rather than as people$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C10Q05$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The gap between what the organisation promises (EVP) and what employees experience in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Minimal — the reality of working here closely matches the promise$qb$, $qb$Small — minor gaps exist but do not significantly affect trust or motivation$qb$, $qb$Significant — there are notable gaps between promise and reality that affect engagement$qb$, $qb$Very large — the EVP and lived experience are so different that it is causing significant disillusionment$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C10Q06$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The quality of exit conversations to understand what would have kept people in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — exit conversations produce deep insight that drives retention improvements$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — useful information is gathered$qb$, $qb$Limited — exit conversations are superficial and produce little actionable insight$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation does not have substantive exit conversations$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C10Q07$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The degree to which the organisation adjusts based on what employees tell it is not working in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — employee input demonstrably influences how the organisation operates$qb$, $qb$Generally present — significant employee concerns are addressed$qb$, $qb$Limited — employee feedback is heard but rarely leads to meaningful change$qb$, $qb$Very limited — employees do not believe their feedback makes any difference$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C10Q08$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The sense that the organisation is investing in employees' futures in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — employees feel the organisation is genuinely invested in their long-term development$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — employees feel the organisation cares about their development$qb$, $qb$Weak — employees feel the organisation takes more than it gives in the employment relationship$qb$, $qb$Very weak — employees feel exploited rather than developed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C10Q09$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The degree to which employees feel positive about remaining with the organisation long-term in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — most employees intend to stay and see a future here$qb$, $qb$Generally positive — more employees intend to stay than to leave$qb$, $qb$Mixed — similar numbers intend to stay and leave$qb$, $qb$Low — most employees do not see a long-term future at the organisation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S18C10Q10$qb$, $qb$S18$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Overall, the talent motivation and retention environment in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is a genuine competitive strength — the organisation is an employer of choice$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — the organisation retains and motivates most of the talent it needs$qb$, $qb$Has notable gaps — motivational and retention deficits are creating talent risk$qb$, $qb$Is a serious concern — poor motivation and retention are threatening the organisation's talent base$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true);
