  ($qb$S19C01Q01$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's customer experience (CX) strategy is:$qb$, $qb$Clearly defined, communicated, and actively used to guide investment and improvement$qb$, $qb$Generally defined — a CX direction exists and is broadly followed$qb$, $qb$Vague — the organisation talks about customer experience without a clear strategy$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — customer experience is managed without a defined strategy$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C01Q02$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Investment in customer experience improvement in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strategic and sustained — CX investment is a defined budget line with measurable outcomes$qb$, $qb$Growing — investment in CX is increasing$qb$, $qb$Limited — some CX investment occurs but it is not comprehensive or sustained$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation does not invest specifically in CX improvement$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C01Q03$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The definition of the target customer experience (what the organisation is trying to achieve for customers) is:$qb$, $qb$Precise and shared — all customer-facing employees know exactly what experience they are trying to deliver$qb$, $qb$Generally understood — the target CX is broadly communicated$qb$, $qb$Vague — the target experience is described in general terms without clarity$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation has not defined what customer experience it is trying to deliver$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C01Q04$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Accountability for the overall customer experience in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Clear — a specific leader or function owns the end-to-end customer experience$qb$, $qb$Generally clear — customer experience ownership is understood$qb$, $qb$Shared but diffuse — multiple functions share responsibility without a clear overall owner$qb$, $qb$Absent — no one is clearly accountable for the overall customer experience$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C01Q05$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Customer experience measurement and reporting to senior leadership in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Regular and comprehensive — CX metrics are on the senior leadership dashboard$qb$, $qb$Periodic — CX performance is reported to leadership periodically$qb$, $qb$Occasional — CX performance reaches leadership only when it becomes a problem$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — senior leadership does not receive regular customer experience reporting$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C01Q06$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's use of customer experience as a competitive differentiator is:$qb$, $qb$Active — CX is explicitly positioned as a competitive advantage$qb$, $qb$Developing — CX is increasingly used as a differentiator$qb$, $qb$Limited — CX is not systematically used as a competitive positioning tool$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation does not position customer experience as a differentiator$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C01Q07$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Customer experience design (deliberate design of customer journeys and touchpoints) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Sophisticated — customer journeys are mapped, designed, and regularly improved$qb$, $qb$Developing — some journey mapping and design is in place$qb$, $qb$Basic — some journey thinking has been done without systematic design$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — customer experience has not been deliberately designed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C01Q08$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+E$qb$, $qb$The integration of CX across all customer-facing functions (sales, service, delivery, billing) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Seamless — all customer-facing functions deliver a consistent and coordinated experience$qb$, $qb$Generally coordinated — most handoffs and touchpoints are managed$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — some touchpoints are excellent; others create friction$qb$, $qb$Poor — fragmented CX delivery creates significant inconsistency for customers$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C01Q09$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's use of customer journey mapping in its management practices is:$qb$, $qb$Active — journeys are mapped, used in management decisions, and regularly updated$qb$, $qb$Periodic — journey mapping has been done and is used in some contexts$qb$, $qb$Limited — some journey mapping work has been done without sustained use$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — customer journey mapping is not used$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C01Q10$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Overall, the customer experience strategy in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is a genuine competitive advantage — the organisation is a CX leader in its market$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — CX strategy provides sufficient direction$qb$, $qb$Has notable gaps — CX strategy weaknesses are limiting the quality of the customer experience$qb$, $qb$Is a serious concern — absence of CX strategy is resulting in poor and inconsistent customer experience$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C02Q01$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The consistency of service quality across all customer touchpoints and channels in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — customers receive a consistently high-quality experience regardless of channel or person$qb$, $qb$Generally consistent — service quality is broadly maintained$qb$, $qb$Variable — quality varies significantly between channels, teams, and individuals$qb$, $qb$Very inconsistent — service quality is highly variable and customers experience significant differences$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C02Q02$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The speed of service delivery in your organisation meets customer expectations:$qb$, $qb$Consistently — the organisation reliably delivers within or ahead of committed timescales$qb$, $qb$Generally — most service is delivered on time$qb$, $qb$Inconsistently — on-time delivery varies significantly$qb$, $qb$Poorly — late delivery is a frequent customer complaint$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C02Q03$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The quality of the end product or service delivered to customers in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — customers consistently receive what they expected at the quality level committed$qb$, $qb$Good — quality is generally high with occasional exceptions$qb$, $qb$Variable — quality is good at times and below standard at others$qb$, $qb$Poor — quality problems are a frequent source of customer dissatisfaction$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C02Q04$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Service delivery processes in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Well-designed, documented, and consistently followed — processes enable reliable, high-quality delivery$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — processes are in place and broadly followed$qb$, $qb$Inconsistently followed — processes exist but individual variation is high$qb$, $qb$Poorly designed or not followed — service delivery quality depends on individual rather than process$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C02Q05$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The accuracy of information provided to customers during service delivery in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — customers receive accurate, complete information at all stages$qb$, $qb$Generally accurate — most information is correct$qb$, $qb$Variable — accuracy depends on the person and channel$qb$, $qb$Poor — inaccurate information is a frequent cause of customer dissatisfaction$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C02Q06$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The proactiveness of communication to customers during delivery (keeping them informed) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — customers are proactively updated at all key stages without needing to ask$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — customers receive adequate communication during delivery$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — proactive communication varies significantly by team or channel$qb$, $qb$Poor — customers regularly have to chase for updates$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C02Q07$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The degree to which front-line employees take personal ownership of customer outcomes in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — front-line employees act as owners of the customer experience$qb$, $qb$Generally present — most front-line employees take reasonable ownership$qb$, $qb$Variable — ownership quality varies significantly across individuals$qb$, $qb$Low — most front-line employees focus on completing tasks rather than owning customer outcomes$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C02Q08$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Service capacity and resource allocation to meet customer demand in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Well-managed — service capacity is proactively managed to meet demand without compromising quality$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — capacity is broadly matched to demand$qb$, $qb$Sometimes insufficient — capacity constraints occasionally degrade service quality$qb$, $qb$Often insufficient — capacity problems are a frequent cause of poor service delivery$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C02Q09$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Error rate and defect rate in service delivery in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Low and declining — quality management processes minimise errors and the trend is improving$qb$, $qb$Acceptable — error rates are within industry norms$qb$, $qb$High — errors are more frequent than they should be$qb$, $qb$Very high — errors and defects are a major and unresolved service quality problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C02Q10$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Overall, service delivery quality and consistency in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is a genuine competitive strength — reliable, high-quality delivery is a recognised advantage$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — service quality broadly meets customer expectations$qb$, $qb$Has notable gaps — service quality inconsistency is creating meaningful customer dissatisfaction$qb$, $qb$Is a serious concern — poor service delivery quality is significantly damaging customer relationships$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C03Q01$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The speed of acknowledging customer complaints in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — complaints are acknowledged within hours$qb$, $qb$Generally good — complaints are acknowledged within 24 hours$qb$, $qb$Variable — acknowledgement time varies significantly$qb$, $qb$Poor — complaints are often not acknowledged promptly$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C03Q02$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The quality of complaint investigation and resolution in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — complaints are investigated thoroughly and resolved fairly in all cases$qb$, $qb$Generally good — most complaints are handled professionally$qb$, $qb$Variable — resolution quality depends on the person handling the complaint$qb$, $qb$Poor — complaints are often resolved poorly or without genuine investigation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C03Q03$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The empathy and humanity of the complaint handling experience in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — customers feel heard and valued during the complaint process$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — complaints are handled professionally if not always warmly$qb$, $qb$Variable — some handlers are empathetic; others are procedural$qb$, $qb$Low — the complaint experience frequently makes customers feel dismissed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C03Q04$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The escalation process for complex or serious complaints in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Well-managed — serious complaints escalate quickly to the right level with adequate authority to resolve$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — escalation works for most serious complaints$qb$, $qb$Slow — escalation processes are not fast enough for the most serious issues$qb$, $qb$Poor — serious complaints are not consistently escalated appropriately$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C03Q05$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$First contact resolution rate (resolving complaints at the first point of contact) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — most complaints are resolved at first contact$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — many complaints are resolved at first contact$qb$, $qb$Low — most complaints require multiple contacts to resolve$qb$, $qb$Very low — first contact resolution is rare and customers must repeatedly follow up$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C03Q06$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The organisation's use of service recovery (turning a complaint into a positive experience) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — when things go wrong, the organisation consistently turns it into a loyalty-building moment$qb$, $qb$Generally good — complaints are resolved in ways that usually retain customers$qb$, $qb$Limited — most complaints are resolved adequately without actively building loyalty$qb$, $qb$Poor — complaint handling often makes the customer relationship worse rather than better$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C03Q07$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Root cause analysis and systemic fixing of recurring complaint causes in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Structured — recurring complaints are systematically analysed and root causes are fixed$qb$, $qb$Generally conducted — significant patterns are identified and addressed$qb$, $qb$Occasional — some pattern analysis occurs without systematic process$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — complaints are resolved individually without systemic root cause analysis$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C03Q08$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The time from complaint to resolution in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Fast — most complaints are resolved within the promised and expected timeframe$qb$, $qb$Generally within commitment — most complaints are resolved within stated timeframes$qb$, $qb$Slow — most complaints take longer to resolve than customers expect$qb$, $qb$Very slow — long complaint resolution times are a significant customer satisfaction problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C03Q09$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's use of complaint data to improve products, services, and processes is:$qb$, $qb$Systematic — complaint data directly drives product, service, and process improvements$qb$, $qb$Generally used — major themes from complaints influence improvement$qb$, $qb$Occasional — some use of complaint data for improvement occurs$qb$, $qb$Not used — complaints are resolved without the insights being used to drive improvement$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C03Q10$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Overall, customer complaint and recovery handling in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is a genuine competitive strength — the organisation turns complaints into loyalty$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — complaints are handled professionally$qb$, $qb$Has notable gaps — complaint handling weaknesses are creating customer relationship risk$qb$, $qb$Is a serious concern — poor complaint handling is damaging customer relationships and reputation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C04Q01$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Customer satisfaction measurement (NPS, CSAT, CES) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Comprehensive and regular — multiple CX metrics are measured across all key touchpoints$qb$, $qb$Adequate — key satisfaction metrics are measured periodically$qb$, $qb$Limited — some measurement exists but it is not comprehensive or regular$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — customer satisfaction is not systematically measured$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C04Q02$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The quality and depth of customer insight gathered in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Deep — both quantitative and qualitative insight is used to build a rich picture of customer needs$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — useful insight is gathered though not always in depth$qb$, $qb$Limited — insight is primarily quantitative without deep qualitative understanding$qb$, $qb$Poor — the organisation has a superficial understanding of its customers$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
