  ($qb$S19C09Q01$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Service operations efficiency (the cost and effort required to deliver service) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — service is delivered efficiently without compromising quality$qb$, $qb$Generally good — operations are efficient with manageable inefficiencies$qb$, $qb$Variable — some operations are efficient; others have known waste or inefficiency$qb$, $qb$Poor — operational inefficiency is significantly increasing costs or compromising quality$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C09Q02$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Process standardisation for service delivery in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — standard processes are documented, followed, and consistently deliver quality outcomes$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — most key processes are standardised$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — standardisation varies significantly across teams and channels$qb$, $qb$Low — processes are largely informal and personal variation is high$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C09Q03$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$First-time-right rate (delivering correctly without rework or correction) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — the vast majority of work is done right the first time$qb$, $qb$Generally good — first-time-right rates are acceptable$qb$, $qb$Variable — first-time-right rates vary significantly$qb$, $qb$Low — rework and correction are common and costly$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C09Q04$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Operational resilience during peak demand in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — the organisation maintains service quality even during peak periods$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — peaks are managed with some degradation$qb$, $qb$Challenged — peak periods regularly cause service quality problems$qb$, $qb$Very challenged — peak demand consistently overwhelms operational capacity$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C09Q05$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Technology enablement of service operations in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — technology significantly enhances operational efficiency and service quality$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — key operational tools are in place$qb$, $qb$Limited — technology support for operations has meaningful gaps$qb$, $qb$Poor — inadequate operational technology is a significant constraint on service quality and efficiency$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C09Q06$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Quality management processes (how errors are prevented, detected, and corrected) in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Comprehensive — proactive quality management reduces errors and the trend is improving$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — quality management processes are in place$qb$, $qb$Partial — some quality management exists without comprehensive coverage$qb$, $qb$Weak — quality management is primarily reactive rather than proactive$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C09Q07$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The integration of back-office and front-office operations for seamless service delivery in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — back and front office are fully integrated enabling seamless service$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — integration works for most service scenarios$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — some integration works well; other handoffs create friction$qb$, $qb$Poor — poor back-front office integration is a frequent cause of service failure$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C09Q08$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Service level agreement (SLA) compliance in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — SLAs are consistently met for all key commitments$qb$, $qb$Generally good — most SLAs are met$qb$, $qb$Variable — SLA compliance varies by service type and volume$qb$, $qb$Poor — SLA compliance is below acceptable levels and is damaging customer satisfaction$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C09Q09$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Continuous improvement of service processes in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Systematic — process improvement is ongoing and produces measurable efficiency and quality gains$qb$, $qb$Active — improvement occurs regularly though not always systematically$qb$, $qb$Occasional — process improvement happens periodically when a problem becomes obvious$qb$, $qb$Absent — processes are not regularly reviewed or improved$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C09Q10$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C09$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$Overall, service operations and process quality in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is a genuine competitive strength — efficient, consistent operations deliver reliable service$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — operations support acceptable service quality$qb$, $qb$Has notable gaps — operational weaknesses are creating quality and efficiency problems$qb$, $qb$Is a serious concern — poor service operations are significantly damaging service quality and commercial performance$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C10Q01$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The comprehensiveness of CX measurement across all touchpoints and channels in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — every significant touchpoint and channel is measured and the data is integrated$qb$, $qb$Good — major touchpoints are measured$qb$, $qb$Adequate — key touchpoints are measured though not comprehensively$qb$, $qb$Poor — CX measurement has significant blind spots$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C10Q02$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+E$qb$, $qb$The quality and accessibility of CX dashboards and reporting in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — CX dashboards are real-time, comprehensive, and accessible to all relevant stakeholders$qb$, $qb$Generally good — CX reporting is adequate and reaches key decision-makers$qb$, $qb$Adequate — basic CX reporting exists but timeliness and accessibility could improve$qb$, $qb$Poor — CX reporting is inadequate for effective management of the customer experience$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C10Q03$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The integration of CX data with other business data (revenue, retention, operations) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — CX and business outcome data are integrated enabling clear demonstration of CX ROI$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — some integration of CX and business data exists$qb$, $qb$Limited — CX and business data are largely separate making ROI demonstration difficult$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — CX data is not integrated with business outcome data$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C10Q04$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The use of predictive analytics for CX (identifying customers likely to churn or escalate) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Active — predictive analytics are used to get ahead of CX problems$qb$, $qb$Developing — some predictive CX analytics are in use$qb$, $qb$Limited — CX analytics are primarily backward-looking$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation does not use predictive analytics for CX management$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C10Q05$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The quality of CX governance (who makes CX decisions and how) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — CX governance is clear, enabling fast decisions and clear accountability$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — CX governance is sufficient$qb$, $qb$Limited — CX decision-making is unclear causing slow response to customer experience issues$qb$, $qb$Poor — CX governance gaps mean important customer experience decisions are not made or made too late$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C10Q06$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The board's visibility of and engagement with customer experience performance in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Regular and substantive — the board reviews CX performance and challenges management on it$qb$, $qb$Periodic — the board receives CX reporting periodically$qb$, $qb$Occasional — CX reaches the board only when it becomes a visible problem$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the board does not receive regular CX reporting$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C10Q07$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The use of CX benchmarking against industry peers in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Regular — performance is benchmarked against industry peers and leaders and gaps are addressed$qb$, $qb$Periodic — some benchmarking occurs$qb$, $qb$Occasional — benchmarking has been done but is not a regular practice$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — CX is not benchmarked against external comparators$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C10Q08$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L+E$qb$, $qb$The organisation's learning from customer experience failures in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Systematic — CX failures are analysed and lessons are applied across the organisation$qb$, $qb$Generally conducted — major CX failures are analysed$qb$, $qb$Occasional — some learning from CX failures occurs$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — CX failures are resolved individually without systemic learning$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C10Q09$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The speed at which the organisation responds to deteriorating CX metrics in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Fast — deteriorating metrics trigger immediate management attention and corrective action$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — deteriorating metrics are addressed within a reasonable timeframe$qb$, $qb$Slow — by the time management responds to deteriorating CX metrics, damage has compounded$qb$, $qb$Very slow — CX metrics must deteriorate significantly before management action is taken$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S19C10Q10$qb$, $qb$S19$qb$, $qb$C10$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Overall, CX measurement, learning, and governance in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is a genuine capability — the organisation understands, manages, and improves CX rigorously$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — CX measurement and governance support effective management$qb$, $qb$Has notable gaps — measurement or governance weaknesses are limiting CX management quality$qb$, $qb$Is insufficient — poor CX measurement and governance are preventing effective management of the customer experience$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true);
INSERT INTO question_bank (question_id, section_code, category_code, level_tag, text, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, score_a, score_b, score_c, score_d, active) VALUES
  ($qb$S20C01Q01$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+E$qb$, $qb$When you step back and assess the overall health of your organisation honestly, it is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — fundamentals are sound across people, strategy, operations, and culture$qb$, $qb$Generally healthy — more strengths than weaknesses with clear improvement areas$qb$, $qb$Mixed — significant strengths exist alongside equally significant weaknesses$qb$, $qb$Under stress — the organisation has more critical weaknesses than strengths right now$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C01Q02$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The biggest organisational strength that gives leadership the most confidence is:$qb$, $qb$Clearly identified, agreed upon, and being actively leveraged as a competitive advantage$qb$, $qb$Known but not fully leveraged or invested in$qb$, $qb$Debated — different leaders have different views on what the real strength is$qb$, $qb$Not identified — leadership has not clearly articulated what the organisation does best$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C01Q03$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The most critical organisational weakness that keeps leadership awake at night is:$qb$, $qb$Clearly identified, owned, and being addressed with a funded, accountable plan$qb$, $qb$Known and discussed but not yet fully addressed$qb$, $qb$Acknowledged at a surface level without deep shared understanding of its root causes$qb$, $qb$Not clearly identified — leadership does not have a shared view of the primary critical weakness$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C01Q04$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The alignment between what the organisation needs to do and what it is actually capable of doing is:$qb$, $qb$High — there is a strong match between strategic ambition and organisational capability$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — most strategic ambitions are within reach$qb$, $qb$Partial — some ambitions exceed current capability without a clear plan to close the gap$qb$, $qb$Significant gap — strategic ambitions are substantially beyond current organisational capability$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C01Q05$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+E$qb$, $qb$The trajectory of organisational health over the last 12 months is:$qb$, $qb$Improving — the organisation is measurably stronger than it was a year ago$qb$, $qb$Stable — the organisation is broadly maintaining its health$qb$, $qb$Mixed — some dimensions are improving while others are declining$qb$, $qb$Declining — overall organisational health has deteriorated over the last 12 months$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C01Q06$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The confidence of the leadership team in the organisation's ability to achieve its goals is:$qb$, $qb$High — leadership is genuinely confident and this confidence is grounded in real capability$qb$, $qb$Adequate — leadership is cautiously optimistic$qb$, $qb$Mixed — some leaders are confident; others are uncertain$qb$, $qb$Low — leadership confidence in the organisation's ability to hit its targets is limited$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C01Q07$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The speed at which the organisation identifies and addresses its own weaknesses is:$qb$, $qb$Fast — problems are identified quickly and addressed decisively$qb$, $qb$Adequate — weaknesses are eventually identified and addressed$qb$, $qb$Slow — the organisation takes longer than it should to recognise and fix weaknesses$qb$, $qb$Very slow — known weaknesses persist for years without adequate action$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C01Q08$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The degree to which the organisation has a clear, shared, honest view of itself in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — leadership and employees share a realistic and honest assessment of where the organisation stands$qb$, $qb$Generally present — there is a reasonable shared understanding$qb$, $qb$Partial — leadership and employees have different views of organisational health$qb$, $qb$Very low — there is significant divergence between the official narrative and the experienced reality$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C01Q09$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$External benchmarking of organisational health (comparing performance to peers) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Regular — the organisation benchmarks itself against peers and uses this to set improvement priorities$qb$, $qb$Periodic — benchmarking occurs but not with sufficient regularity$qb$, $qb$Occasional — benchmarking has been done but is not a systematic practice$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation does not benchmark its health against external peers$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C01Q10$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C01$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Overall, the current organisational health of your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is strong — the organisation is well-positioned to achieve its ambitions$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — the organisation is functional with clear improvement priorities$qb$, $qb$Is under pressure — significant areas need urgent improvement$qb$, $qb$Is in need of serious intervention — multiple critical areas are at risk simultaneously$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C02Q01$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The urgency that leadership feels about the need for organisational transformation is:$qb$, $qb$High — leadership understands that significant change is needed now to remain competitive$qb$, $qb$Moderate — leadership recognises change is needed but the pace is not urgent$qb$, $qb$Low — leadership believes the current approach is broadly working and change can wait$qb$, $qb$Very low — leadership is largely satisfied with the status quo$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C02Q02$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The scale of transformation the organisation needs in your leadership's view is:$qb$, $qb$Fundamental — the organisation needs to change how it works across multiple dimensions$qb$, $qb$Significant — several important areas need serious improvement$qb$, $qb$Moderate — targeted improvements are needed in specific areas$qb$, $qb$Minimal — incremental refinements are sufficient$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C02Q03$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The organisation's track record of successfully implementing significant transformation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — the organisation has delivered multiple successful large-scale transformations$qb$, $qb$Generally positive — transformations have mostly achieved their objectives$qb$, $qb$Mixed — some transformations have succeeded; others have failed or stalled$qb$, $qb$Poor — the organisation has a history of transformation initiatives that did not achieve their goals$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C02Q04$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The degree to which transformation is currently a funded, governed priority in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — transformation is a defined strategic programme with budget, governance, and executive ownership$qb$, $qb$Moderate — some transformation activity is funded and in progress$qb$, $qb$Low — transformation is discussed but not yet a funded and governed priority$qb$, $qb$Very low — transformation is aspirational without any current structure or investment behind it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C02Q05$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Employee readiness for significant organisational change in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — employees understand the need for change and are ready to engage with it$qb$, $qb$Moderate — most employees accept that change is needed$qb$, $qb$Mixed — some employees are ready; others are resistant or disengaged$qb$, $qb$Low — significant employee resistance or change fatigue is likely to impede transformation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C02Q06$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The quality of change management capability the organisation has to execute transformation in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — the organisation has proven change management expertise it can deploy$qb$, $qb$Adequate — basic change management capability is available$qb$, $qb$Limited — change management capability is a gap that would need to be built or bought$qb$, $qb$Very limited — the organisation has poor change management capability and this is a major transformation risk$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C02Q07$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The degree to which the organisation has a clear vision of what it will look like post-transformation is:$qb$, $qb$Crystal clear — the future state is vividly described and widely understood$qb$, $qb$Generally defined — the destination is described at a high level$qb$, $qb$Partially defined — some aspects of the future state are clear; others are vague$qb$, $qb$Not defined — the organisation knows it needs to change without clarity on what it is changing toward$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C02Q08$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The degree to which leaders are personally prepared to change their own behaviour as part of transformation is:$qb$, $qb$High — leadership has acknowledged that transformation starts with them and is personally committed$qb$, $qb$Moderate — most leaders are willing to change what is needed$qb$, $qb$Variable — some leaders are personally committed; others expect others to change$qb$, $qb$Low — leadership transformation readiness is a significant risk to the success of any transformation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C02Q09$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The time horizon over which transformation is expected to produce visible results in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Short (6-12 months) — quick wins are expected alongside longer-term structural change$qb$, $qb$Medium (1-2 years) — meaningful results are expected within 2 years$qb$, $qb$Long (2-3 years) — the organisation is prepared to invest with a longer return horizon$qb$, $qb$Unclear — expectations about transformation timeline are not well-defined$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C02Q10$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C02$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Overall, the transformation readiness and urgency of your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is high — the organisation is ready and committed to meaningful transformation$qb$, $qb$Is building — readiness is developing and some important foundations are in place$qb$, $qb$Is limited — significant readiness gaps would slow any transformation effort$qb$, $qb$Is very low — the organisation is not yet ready for the transformation it needs$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C03Q01$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The senior leadership team's collective commitment to driving transformation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong and unified — all leaders are personally committed and aligned on the need for change$qb$, $qb$Generally strong — most leaders are committed with some variation$qb$, $qb$Mixed — commitment varies significantly across the leadership team$qb$, $qb$Weak — leadership commitment to transformation is insufficient to drive it$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C03Q02$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The quality of transformational leadership capability in the senior team in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — senior leaders have the skills, mindset, and experience to lead complex transformation$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — leadership capability is broadly sufficient$qb$, $qb$Patchy — some leaders have strong transformation capability; others have significant gaps$qb$, $qb$Low — transformational leadership capability is a critical gap$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C03Q03$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The willingness of senior leaders to make difficult decisions required for transformation in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — leaders are willing to make tough calls including on people, structure, and investment$qb$, $qb$Generally present — difficult decisions are made when they are clearly necessary$qb$, $qb$Cautious — difficult decisions are often delayed or softened beyond what transformation requires$qb$, $qb$Low — risk aversion or conflict avoidance prevents the leadership team from making necessary transformation decisions$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C03Q04$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The degree to which the CEO or MD is personally championing transformation in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — the top leader is the most visible and committed advocate for change$qb$, $qb$Generally present — top leadership support is evident$qb$, $qb$Moderate — top leadership support exists but is not as personal or visible as transformation requires$qb$, $qb$Weak — top leader support for transformation is insufficient to drive the required momentum$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C03Q05$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The degree to which middle management is ready to support and implement transformation in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — middle managers understand the transformation agenda and are equipped to lead change in their areas$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — most middle managers are supportive$qb$, $qb$Mixed — some middle managers are champions; others are resistant or disengaged$qb$, $qb$Low — middle management resistance is a significant risk to transformation success$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C03Q06$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Leadership team stability during transformation in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — the team is stable and committed for the duration of the transformation$qb$, $qb$Generally stable — the team is likely to remain in place through the key transformation period$qb$, $qb$Some uncertainty — one or more key leadership changes during transformation are possible$qb$, $qb$Significant uncertainty — leadership instability is a real risk to transformation continuity$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C03Q07$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+E$qb$, $qb$The degree to which leaders are modelling the change they are asking others to make in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Consistently — leaders are visibly changing their own behaviour in line with the transformation$qb$, $qb$Generally — most leaders are modelling the expected changes$qb$, $qb$Inconsistently — some leaders model the change; others continue in old patterns$qb$, $qb$Poorly — leaders are asking for change without changing themselves$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C03Q08$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+E$qb$, $qb$Leadership communication about the transformation narrative (why change, what change, what it means for people) is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — the narrative is compelling, honest, and consistently communicated$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — the narrative is communicated with reasonable effectiveness$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — the narrative is not consistently or compellingly told$qb$, $qb$Weak — employees do not have a clear understanding of the transformation story$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C03Q09$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The investment in developing leaders' own transformation capability in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Active — leaders are receiving specific support to build their own transformation leadership skills$qb$, $qb$Some — some leadership development around transformation is occurring$qb$, $qb$Limited — leadership development for transformation is not a current priority$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — leaders are expected to lead transformation without specific development support$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C03Q10$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C03$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Overall, leadership readiness for transformation in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is a genuine strength — leadership is the engine of the transformation$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — leadership is broadly capable of driving transformation$qb$, $qb$Has notable gaps — leadership readiness gaps are a significant transformation risk$qb$, $qb$Is a critical risk — insufficient leadership readiness would likely cause any transformation to fail$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C04Q01$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The organisation's current capacity to absorb additional change on top of BAU in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — the organisation has real bandwidth to take on significant transformation$qb$, $qb$Moderate — some capacity exists but the organisation is already managing significant change$qb$, $qb$Limited — the organisation is at or near capacity and additional change would be absorbed poorly$qb$, $qb$Very limited — change fatigue is already present and the organisation cannot absorb significant additional change$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C04Q02$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The degree to which the organisation can maintain business performance while simultaneously transforming in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — the organisation can run the business and transform it simultaneously$qb$, $qb$Adequate — performance can be maintained with some dedicated transformation resource$qb$, $qb$Challenging — the organisation struggles to perform and transform at the same time$qb$, $qb$Very challenging — the organisation has consistently failed to sustain performance during transformation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C04Q03$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Dedicated resource available for transformation (people not consumed by BAU who can drive change) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Sufficient — dedicated transformation resource is available or can be released$qb$, $qb$Partially sufficient — some dedicated resource is available but not fully what is needed$qb$, $qb$Limited — the organisation is stretched and freeing resource for transformation is difficult$qb$, $qb$Very limited — virtually everyone is absorbed in BAU and there is no real capacity for transformation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C04Q04$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The number of concurrent major initiatives the organisation is currently running in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Well-managed — the initiative portfolio is disciplined and manageable$qb$, $qb$Adequate — the number of major initiatives is at the edge of what the organisation can manage$qb$, $qb$Too many — the organisation is trying to do too many things at once and execution quality is suffering$qb$, $qb$Dangerously high — initiative overload is causing multiple programmes to underperform$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C04Q05$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$Employee energy and appetite for the next transformation initiative in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — employees would welcome a well-designed transformation$qb$, $qb$Moderate — employees are open to change if it is well-led$qb$, $qb$Tired — change fatigue is visible and employee appetite for further transformation is limited$qb$, $qb$Very low — employees are exhausted and any further transformation would face significant resistance$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C04Q06$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The speed at which the organisation can mobilise for a transformation effort in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Fast — within weeks, a capable transformation team and governance structure could be stood up$qb$, $qb$Moderate — mobilisation would take a quarter$qb$, $qb$Slow — mobilisation would take 6 months or more$qb$, $qb$Very slow — the organisation has no playbook for mobilising transformation and it would take a long time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C04Q07$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The quality of project and programme management capability available for transformation in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — the organisation has the PM and programme management skills to run a complex transformation$qb$, $qb$Adequate — basic PM capability is available$qb$, $qb$Limited — PM capability for large-scale transformation would need to be significantly supplemented$qb$, $qb$Very limited — programme management capability is a critical gap for any significant transformation$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C04Q08$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Financial capacity to fund transformation investment in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — the organisation can fund the transformation required without financial stress$qb$, $qb$Adequate — transformation investment is possible within current financial capacity$qb$, $qb$Limited — transformation investment would require difficult trade-offs or external funding$qb$, $qb$Very limited — financial constraints are a major barrier to the transformation the organisation needs$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C04Q09$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The organisation's ability to prioritise ruthlessly (stopping things to free capacity for transformation) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — the organisation can make difficult stop decisions to create transformation bandwidth$qb$, $qb$Moderate — some stop decisions can be made but it is not easy$qb$, $qb$Low — the organisation finds it very difficult to stop existing activities to create transformation space$qb$, $qb$Very low — the organisation has never successfully stopped significant activities to create capacity$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C04Q10$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C04$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Overall, change capacity and bandwidth for transformation in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is sufficient — the organisation has the capacity to drive meaningful transformation now$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — with some trade-offs, transformation is feasible$qb$, $qb$Is constrained — capacity limitations would need to be actively managed for transformation to succeed$qb$, $qb$Is a critical barrier — insufficient capacity would likely undermine any transformation effort$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C05Q01$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The clarity of the organisation's view of its top 3 transformation priorities is:$qb$, $qb$Very clear — the top 3 priorities are agreed, explicitly stated, and backed by analysis$qb$, $qb$Generally clear — priorities are broadly agreed with some variation in emphasis$qb$, $qb$Partially clear — there is agreement on some priorities but debate about others$qb$, $qb$Unclear — the organisation does not have a shared view of its top transformation priorities$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C05Q02$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM$qb$, $qb$The alignment of transformation priorities across the leadership team in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — all leaders agree on what the organisation should focus on first$qb$, $qb$Generally aligned — most leaders agree on the major priorities$qb$, $qb$Partially aligned — some priorities are agreed; others are contested$qb$, $qb$Misaligned — different leaders have significantly different views on what matters most$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C05Q03$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The evidence base for identified transformation priorities (data, diagnosis, customer feedback) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — priorities are clearly evidenced and the root cause analysis is robust$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — reasonable evidence supports the main priorities$qb$, $qb$Partial — some priorities are well-evidenced; others are based primarily on intuition$qb$, $qb$Weak — transformation priorities are largely based on leadership intuition without deep evidence$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C05Q04$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The degree to which employee experience data is informing transformation priorities in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Significantly — employee experience data is a major input into priority identification$qb$, $qb$Somewhat — employee data is considered alongside other inputs$qb$, $qb$Minimally — employee data is collected but does not significantly influence priority setting$qb$, $qb$Not at all — transformation priorities are set without meaningful employee experience data$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C05Q05$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's approach to prioritisation (choosing what NOT to do) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Disciplined — the organisation is willing to deprioritise activities that do not align with transformation$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — some prioritisation discipline exists$qb$, $qb$Weak — the organisation tends to add priorities without removing others$qb$, $qb$Very weak — the organisation has no effective prioritisation discipline and tries to do everything$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C05Q06$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The degree to which frontline and customer-facing perspectives have shaped transformation priorities in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strongly — frontline and customer perspectives are a primary input into priority setting$qb$, $qb$Adequately — frontline input is included in the priority-setting process$qb$, $qb$Minimally — frontline perspectives have had limited influence on the priorities identified$qb$, $qb$Not at all — priorities were set by leadership without meaningful frontline or customer input$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C05Q07$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The speed with which agreed transformation priorities are translated into funded action in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Fast — priorities become funded programmes within weeks of being agreed$qb$, $qb$Moderate — funded action follows agreed priorities within a quarter$qb$, $qb$Slow — translating agreed priorities into funded action typically takes 6-12 months$qb$, $qb$Very slow — agreed priorities often stay as intentions without becoming funded action$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C05Q08$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L+MM+E$qb$, $qb$The organisation's track record of maintaining focus on stated priorities through the year in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — stated priorities remain the focus throughout the year without significant drift$qb$, $qb$Generally maintained — priorities are broadly maintained$qb$, $qb$Inconsistent — stated priorities are often crowded out by reactive demands$qb$, $qb$Poor — the organisation consistently loses focus on stated priorities as the year progresses$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C05Q09$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The quality of transformation roadmap (sequenced plan of what happens when) in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Excellent — a detailed, realistic roadmap exists with clear milestones and dependencies$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — a roadmap exists at a high level$qb$, $qb$Basic — a rough sequence of priorities is understood without a detailed roadmap$qb$, $qb$Non-existent — the organisation has identified priorities but not sequenced them into a roadmap$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C05Q10$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C05$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Overall, the clarity and quality of transformation priority identification in your organisation:$qb$, $qb$Is excellent — the organisation knows exactly what to work on and in what sequence$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — priorities are identified with reasonable clarity$qb$, $qb$Has notable gaps — priority clarity problems would slow transformation$qb$, $qb$Is insufficient — poor priority identification would cause the transformation to lack focus and direction$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C06Q01$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+E$qb$, $qb$The leadership team's openness to bringing in external expertise to support transformation in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — leadership actively welcomes external perspective and challenge$qb$, $qb$Generally open — leadership is willing to use external support when the need is clear$qb$, $qb$Cautious — there is some resistance to external involvement based on past experience or culture$qb$, $qb$Low — leadership prefers to manage transformation internally and is resistant to external advisory support$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C06Q02$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L+E$qb$, $qb$Past experience with external consultants in your organisation has been:$qb$, $qb$Very positive — previous consulting engagements have delivered real value$qb$, $qb$Generally positive — most engagements have been worthwhile$qb$, $qb$Mixed — some engagements have been valuable; others have not$qb$, $qb$Negative — poor past experience has made leadership sceptical of external consulting value$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C06Q03$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The degree to which leadership is seeking genuine challenge (not validation) from an external advisor in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — leadership is specifically seeking honest external assessment and challenge$qb$, $qb$Generally open to challenge — leadership expects honest feedback$qb$, $qb$Moderately open — leadership wants support but may be resistant to findings that challenge their view$qb$, $qb$Looking for validation — leadership primarily wants external support that confirms their existing views$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C06Q04$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The willingness to share sensitive internal data and honest assessments with an external partner in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — leadership will share what is needed to make the engagement genuinely impactful$qb$, $qb$Generally willing — most relevant information will be made available$qb$, $qb$Cautious — some sensitivity about what is shared would limit the depth of engagement$qb$, $qb$Very cautious — limited information-sharing would significantly constrain what an advisor can deliver$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C06Q05$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The internal stakeholder whose buy-in is most critical for any consulting engagement to succeed in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Clearly identified and personally committed to the engagement$qb$, $qb$Identified — the key stakeholder is known and broadly supportive$qb$, $qb$Partially identified — some key stakeholders are identified and supportive but others are uncertain$qb$, $qb$Not clearly identified — stakeholder mapping for an engagement has not been done$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C06Q06$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's preferred model for working with external consultants (advice only, hands-on delivery, embedded) is:$qb$, $qb$Clear — leadership has a well-defined view of how it wants to engage with advisors$qb$, $qb$Generally understood — preferences are broadly known$qb$, $qb$Partially formed — preferences are not fully thought through$qb$, $qb$Not formed — the organisation has not considered what kind of engagement it is looking for$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C06Q07$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The timeline for which external support is needed in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Defined — a clear timeline is in mind for when support is needed to start$qb$, $qb$Generally understood — a broad timeframe is envisioned$qb$, $qb$Flexible — the organisation is open to being advised on timing$qb$, $qb$Not yet considered — the timeline for engaging external support has not been thought about$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C06Q08$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The budget currently allocated or expected for external transformation support in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Defined and approved — budget for external support has been allocated$qb$, $qb$In process — budget allocation is under discussion$qb$, $qb$Under consideration — the question of budget has been raised without resolution$qb$, $qb$Not yet discussed — no budget for external support has been considered$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C06Q09$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The specific outcomes the organisation wants from an external consulting engagement in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Clearly defined — specific, measurable outcomes are agreed for any engagement$qb$, $qb$Generally understood — the desired outcomes are broadly clear$qb$, $qb$Partially formed — some outcomes are identified but others are vague$qb$, $qb$Not defined — the organisation has not clearly articulated what it wants from an external engagement$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C06Q10$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C06$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$Overall, the organisation's openness to and readiness for external consulting support:$qb$, $qb$Is high — the organisation is ready to engage and there is strong leadership buy-in$qb$, $qb$Is adequate — there is genuine openness to external support$qb$, $qb$Is mixed — openness exists at some levels but resistance or uncertainty at others could impede engagement$qb$, $qb$Is low — significant barriers to productive external engagement would need to be addressed first$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C07Q01$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's current financial position relative to transformation investment requirements is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — the organisation can fund the transformation it needs without significant financial stress$qb$, $qb$Adequate — transformation investment is feasible within current capacity with some trade-offs$qb$, $qb$Constrained — the budget available for transformation is below what is likely to be required$qb$, $qb$Very constrained — financial limitations are a primary barrier to the transformation the organisation needs$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C07Q02$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The appetite of the board and shareholders for transformation investment in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — there is strong board and shareholder support for investing in transformation$qb$, $qb$Generally supportive — the board and shareholders broadly support necessary investment$qb$, $qb$Mixed — some board members or shareholders are supportive; others are resistant$qb$, $qb$Low — board and shareholder resistance to investment is a significant constraint$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C07Q03$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's ability to quantify the financial return of transformation investment in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Strong — the business case for transformation has been or can be clearly quantified$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — a credible business case can be constructed$qb$, $qb$Limited — the financial return of transformation is difficult to quantify with confidence$qb$, $qb$Very limited — the organisation struggles to make a financial case for transformation investment$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C07Q04$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L+E$qb$, $qb$The degree to which transformation investment is protected from budget cuts during difficult periods in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$Well-protected — transformation investment is ring-fenced and not subject to the same scrutiny as operational spend$qb$, $qb$Generally protected — transformation investment is maintained unless financial stress becomes extreme$qb$, $qb$Vulnerable — transformation investment is one of the first things cut when financial pressure increases$qb$, $qb$Very vulnerable — there is no protection for transformation investment under financial pressure$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C07Q05$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L$qb$, $qb$The organisation's ROI expectations for transformation in your organisation are:$qb$, $qb$Realistic — leadership has a grounded view of what transformation can achieve and over what timeframe$qb$, $qb$Generally realistic — expectations are broadly reasonable$qb$, $qb$Optimistic — expectations may be higher than what is achievable in the stated timeframe$qb$, $qb$Very optimistic — unrealistic expectations could lead to premature termination of transformation effort$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
  ($qb$S20C07Q06$qb$, $qb$S20$qb$, $qb$C07$qb$, $qb$L+E$qb$, $qb$The organisation's willingness to invest in people capability as part of transformation in your organisation is:$qb$, $qb$High — investment in people capability is seen as essential to transformation success$qb$, $qb$Generally adequate — some people investment is expected as part of transformation$qb$, $qb$Limited — people investment is seen as a cost rather than a transformation enabler$qb$, $qb$Very limited — leadership is resistant to the level of people investment that transformation typically requires$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, true),
